-- A valid property with a history quantifier nested inside another quantifier
-- (issue #1721). Its body holds for every `k`, so it simplifies to true, and
-- the history array it mentioned is sliced away: the system IC3IA checks the
-- property on is quantified, but has no array, so the check that declines
-- quantified formulas over arrays up front, which the same property with the
-- history quantifier at the top level runs into, rightly lets it through. The
-- refinement of the abstraction then went through MathSAT, which does not
-- support quantifiers and does not reject them either: it answered with a
-- model that violates the quantified definition, and IC3IA reported the
-- property invalid after 1 step. IC3IA must not claim it is falsifiable:
-- declining to answer (and leaving it to the other engines) is the expected
-- outcome with MathSAT, and the interpolating solvers that support
-- quantifiers prove it.
node N(x: int) returns ();
let
  check "P1" forall (k: int) exists (z: history(x)) z = x or k = k;
tel
